1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,457
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,457 --> 00:00:04,460
Episode 1.

3
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:07,073
The Lunchbox Detective.

4
00:00:07,073 --> 00:00:09,914
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,914 --> 00:00:15,298
What finally helped the children find the owners: the color or the labels?

6
00:00:15,298 --> 00:00:16,789
Ready?

7
00:00:16,789 --> 00:00:18,963
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,963 --> 00:00:23,678
At Maple School, Maya puts a blue lunchbox on the table.

9
00:00:23,678 --> 00:00:27,414
Leo puts another blue lunchbox beside it.

10
00:00:27,414 --> 00:00:30,156
They look exactly the same.

11
00:00:30,156 --> 00:00:32,130
Is this yours?

12
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:34,247
Maya asks.

13
00:00:34,247 --> 00:00:37,343
I think so, Leo says.

14
00:00:37,343 --> 00:00:39,389
He opens it.

15
00:00:39,389 --> 00:00:43,352
Inside are rice, carrots, and an egg.

16
00:00:43,352 --> 00:00:45,497
That is strange.

17
00:00:45,497 --> 00:00:48,323
My dad made me a sandwich.

18
00:00:48,323 --> 00:00:51,548
Then this one is mine, says Maya.

19
00:00:51,548 --> 00:00:53,309
But wait.

20
00:00:53,309 --> 00:00:55,951
My lunch has noodles today.

21
00:00:55,951 --> 00:01:00,582
Now they have two lunchboxes and no lunch they recognize.

22
00:01:00,582 --> 00:01:04,786
Pip, their small classroom robot, rolls over.

23
00:01:04,786 --> 00:01:07,002
I have a solution.

24
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:09,332
Eat both lunches.

25
00:01:09,332 --> 00:01:12,727
That is not a solution, says Maya.

26
00:01:12,727 --> 00:01:15,241
That is your lunch plan.

27
00:01:15,241 --> 00:01:19,829
Ms. Park asks them to look for clues without eating anything.

28
00:01:19,829 --> 00:01:23,352
One box has a tiny star on the bottom.

29
00:01:23,352 --> 00:01:26,235
The other has a name under the handle.

30
00:01:26,235 --> 00:01:29,346
This says Sam, Leo reads.

31
00:01:29,346 --> 00:01:31,519
So it is his.

32
00:01:31,519 --> 00:01:34,232
A girl named June arrives.

33
00:01:34,232 --> 00:01:37,329
The star is mine, she says.

34
00:01:37,329 --> 00:01:39,517
My brother drew it.

35
00:01:39,517 --> 00:01:42,144
Maya looks under the table.

36
00:01:42,144 --> 00:01:46,448
There is her lunchbox, still inside her backpack.

37
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:49,403
Leo checks his bag, too.

38
00:01:49,403 --> 00:01:51,775
His sandwich is there.

39
00:01:51,775 --> 00:01:57,528
We were looking at their lunches, says Maya, but we did not check our own bags.

40
00:01:57,528 --> 00:01:59,943
Pip thinks for a moment.

41
00:01:59,943 --> 00:02:03,508
My first clue is that I am still hungry.

42
00:02:03,508 --> 00:02:07,343
You do not eat sandwiches, Leo tells him.

43
00:02:07,343 --> 00:02:09,928
Correct, says Pip.

44
00:02:09,928 --> 00:02:12,982
But I can appreciate a good mystery.

45
00:02:12,982 --> 00:02:16,022
Now, a little more to discover.

46
00:02:16,022 --> 00:02:19,715
A clue is something that helps you answer a question.

47
00:02:19,715 --> 00:02:22,343
Evidence supports an idea.

48
00:02:22,343 --> 00:02:27,144
A blue box is a weak clue when many people have blue boxes.

49
00:02:27,144 --> 00:02:30,369
A name label is stronger evidence.

50
00:02:30,369 --> 00:02:35,014
Good detectives check their ideas before deciding they are right.

51
00:02:35,014 --> 00:02:37,982
Let us hear the main idea again.

52
00:02:37,982 --> 00:02:43,139
Maya and Leo saw two blue lunchboxes and guessed they were theirs.

53
00:02:43,139 --> 00:02:47,173
Labels showed that the boxes belonged to Sam and June.

54
00:02:47,173 --> 00:02:50,582
Their own lunches were still in their bags.

55
00:02:50,582 --> 00:02:53,707
Looking carefully solved the mystery.

56
00:02:53,707 --> 00:02:56,818
Here are three useful expressions.

57
00:02:56,818 --> 00:03:00,085
You can listen, or say them with me.

58
00:03:00,085 --> 00:03:02,059
Is this yours?

59
00:03:02,059 --> 00:03:04,034
Is this yours?

60
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:06,846
No, that one is mine.

61
00:03:06,846 --> 00:03:09,659
No, that one is mine.

62
00:03:09,659 --> 00:03:12,187
Let us check the evidence.

63
00:03:12,187 --> 00:03:14,715
Let us check the evidence.

64
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:17,428
Remember our listening question?

65
00:03:17,428 --> 00:03:22,812
What finally helped the children find the owners: the color or the labels?

66
00:03:22,812 --> 00:03:25,042
Here is the answer.

67
00:03:25,042 --> 00:03:27,372
The labels helped them.

68
00:03:27,372 --> 00:03:32,116
Both boxes were blue, so color alone was not enough.

69
00:03:32,116 --> 00:03:35,937
And here is a question just for your imagination.

70
00:03:35,937 --> 00:03:41,789
If you had to describe your backpack without using its color, what clues would you give?

71
00:03:41,789 --> 00:03:45,042
There is more than one possible answer.

72
00:03:45,042 --> 00:03:48,437
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
